WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:20.932
My curiosity was stronger than my fear, so I crept back toward the inn, hid behind a bush, and watched as my enemies began to arrive.

2
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:25.511
Five of them ran along the road, with a man holding a lantern in front.

3
00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:31.866
Right behind them were three men, hand in hand; the man in the middle was blind.

4
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:35.965
"Knock down the door!" the blind man cried.

5
00:00:35.966 --> 00:00:41.160
"Aye, aye, sir!" two men answered, and then they ran at the door.

6
00:00:41.161 --> 00:00:46.627
I saw them pause in confusion and surprise when they discovered that the door was open.

7
00:00:46.628 --> 00:00:50.825
Then the blind man shouted, "Go in! In! In!"

8
00:00:50.826 --> 00:00:54.164
He cursed at the men for their delay.

9
00:00:54.165 --> 00:00:59.170
Five of them obeyed at once while two stayed in the road with the blind man.

10
00:00:59.171 --> 00:01:05.983
There was a cry of surprise, and then someone shouted from the house, "Bill's dead!"

11
00:01:05.984 --> 00:01:11.297
The blind man swore again. "Search him, and then get the chest!"

12
00:01:11.298 --> 00:01:17.290
I could hear their feet rattling up our stairs; they made the whole house shake.

13
00:01:17.291 --> 00:01:23.433
The window of the captain's room was thrown open with a slam and a jingle of broken glass.

14
00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:28.997
A man leaned out into the moonlight and addressed the blind man on the road below.

15
00:01:28.998 --> 00:01:34.680
"Pew," he cried, "someone got here first. The chest is open."

16
00:01:34.681 --> 00:01:38.984
"Did you find them?" roared Pew, the blind man.

17
00:01:38.985 --> 00:01:41.660
"Some money's there."

18
00:01:41.661 --> 00:01:48.218
"I don't care about the money, you swab," cursed Pew. "Flint's secret papers, I mean."

19
00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:53.464
"We don't see anything that looks like that," the man replied.

20
00:01:53.465 --> 00:01:59.352
"You, below there, are the papers on Bill?" Pew cried to the men on the first floor.

21
00:01:59.353 --> 00:02:02.929
At that, another fellow came to the door of the inn.

22
00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:07.816
"Bill's been searched already," he said. "Nothing's left."

23
00:02:07.817 --> 00:02:13.525
"It's those inn people—it's that boy. I wish I'd put his eyes out!" Pew cried.

24
00:02:13.526 --> 00:02:18.576
"They can't be far. Scatter, lads. Find them!"

25
00:02:18.577 --> 00:02:25.361
Heavy feet pounded to and fro, and the men toppled furniture over and kicked in the doors of every room.

26
00:02:25.362 --> 00:02:30.291
Then the men came out again, one after another, onto the road.

27
00:02:30.292 --> 00:02:34.427
"They aren't here," one announced.

28
00:02:34.428 --> 00:02:39.528
The whistle that had frightened my mother and me earlier sounded two times.

29
00:02:39.529 --> 00:02:44.482
Then, I had thought it was Pew calling his men to assault us,

30
00:02:44.483 --> 00:02:50.359
but now, I realized it was a warning signal from a lookout hiding somewhere near the village.

31
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.908
"There's the signal," a man said.

32
00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:57.476
"That means the law is coming. Let's go!"

33
00:02:57.477 --> 00:03:00.911
"You dogs, you're so close," cried Pew.

34
00:03:00.912 --> 00:03:06.328
"Scatter and look for Bill's secret! Oh, if I only had eyes!"

35
00:03:06.329 --> 00:03:13.985
At Pew's urging, two fellows began to look here and there, but half-heartedly, with an eye to their own danger all the time.

36
00:03:13.986 --> 00:03:18.802
The rest stood on the road, unsure what to do.

37
00:03:18.803 --> 00:03:23.243
"You could be as rich as kings, you fools!" Pew shouted.

38
00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:26.332
"But you're just standing around!

39
00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:30.275
I'll end up a poor beggar, when I could be riding in a fine coach!

40
00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:34.004
If you had any guts, you'd catch that boy."

41
00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:38.630
"Forget it, Pew. We've got the money!" grumbled one man.

42
00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:42.543
"The boy and his parents might have hid the papers," another said.

43
00:03:42.544 --> 00:03:47.549
"Take the money, Pew, and don't stand here squawking."

44
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:53.192
Pew struck blindly at his men right and left, with his stick hitting a few of them.

45
00:03:53.193 --> 00:04:00.123
They, in turn, cursed at the sightless villain and tried in vain to catch the stick and take it from him.

46
00:04:00.124 --> 00:04:05.982
Their quarrel saved my mother and me, for while it was raging, there was another sound—horses galloping.

47
00:04:05.983 --> 00:04:15.523
The pirates heard it too. They turned and ran, separating in every direction so that in half a minute no one remained but Pew.

48
00:04:15.524 --> 00:04:20.285
He tapped up and down the road in a frenzy, groping and calling.

49
00:04:20.286 --> 00:04:35.345
Finally he took a wrong turn and ran a few steps past me, crying, "Alan, Tom, Job," and other names, "don't leave old Pew, mates—not old Pew!"

50
00:04:35.346 --> 00:04:44.122
The noise of the horses grew louder, and five riders swept down the road at a full gallop. Pew screamed and ran straight into a ditch.

51
00:04:44.123 --> 00:04:52.571
But he was on his feet again in a second and made another dash, now utterly bewildered, right into the path of the nearest horse.

52
00:04:52.572 --> 00:04:57.016
The rider tried to stop, but Pew went down with a terrible cry.

53
00:04:57.017 --> 00:05:05.176
The four hoofs trampled him as he fell on his side and then collapsed face down, moving no more.

54
00:05:05.177 --> 00:05:10.254
As the riders pulled up, I leaped to my feet, horrified at the accident.

55
00:05:10.255 --> 00:05:14.239
A lad from the village was trailing behind the horsemen.

56
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.277
"These men are military officers," he told me.

57
00:05:17.278 --> 00:05:21.935
"I met them and recruited them to help on my way to Dr. Livesey's."

58
00:05:21.936 --> 00:05:27.874
Pew was dead, and his crew had fled back to their ship, chased by the horsemen.

59
00:05:27.875 --> 00:05:34.716
As for my mother, we carried her to the village, and cold water and smelling salts soon had her back to normal.

60
00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:44.293
When I finally returned to the Admiral Benbow with Captain Dance, one of the officers, I saw that the house was completely wrecked.

61
00:05:44.294 --> 00:05:50.247
"We are ruined!" I cried, looking at the result of the pirates' furious hunt.

62
00:05:50.248 --> 00:05:54.927
Captain Dance was sympathetic. "What were they after? Money?"

63
00:05:54.928 --> 00:05:57.261
"No, sir, not money," I replied.

64
00:05:57.262 --> 00:06:00.169
"I believe I have what they were looking for in my breast pocket.

65
00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.725
I'd like to show it to Dr. Livesey."

66
00:06:03.726 --> 00:06:10.433
"Well, I have to ride there and report what happened back to him and the squire," he said cheerily as he mounted his horse.

67
00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:13.198
"So come along, Hawkins."

68
00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:22.499
"Thank you, sir," I said as he offered his hand and then pulled me up behind him in the saddle.
